window.docContents[91].resolve({"/Constructions/#section-kneser-graphs":{"contents":"The k-th Kneser graph M. Kneser, Aufgabe 360 [Problem 360], Jahresber. Deutsch. Math.-Verein. 58 (1955), no. 2, 27. See also Martin Kneser, Collected Works, Springer, 2021, 729–742. on a type β has as vertices the\nk-element subsets of β, with edges between any distinct disjoint subsets. Before\nwe can define this as a SimpleGraph we need to decide what its vertex type should\nbe. If we are happy to work with finite types (i.e. to assume Fintype β) then we\ncould use (Finset.univ : Finset β).powersetCard k which is the finite family of\nk-element subsets of a finite type β.\n\n#check Finset.powersetCard\n\n\nUsing Finset.powersetCard gives us easy access to its API consisting of counting\nresults about families of finite sets, which would be useful.\n\n@[simps]\nprivate def kneserGraphFintype (β : Type*) [Fintype β] (k : ℕ) :\n    SimpleGraph ((Finset.univ : Finset β).powersetCard k) where\n  Adj u v := Disjoint u.val v.val ∧ u ≠ v\n  symm := ⟨fun _ _ h => ⟨h.1.symm, h.2.symm⟩⟩\n\n\nBut there are two drawbacks with this definition. Firstly, the finiteness assumption\nis a bit restrictive. Secondly, this vertex type mentions Finset.univ, and hence\nthe Fintype β instance: two different instances give vertex types that are equal\nbut not definitionally equal, so a lemma proved for one does not apply to the other.\n(Since we won't actually use this definition we marked it private.)\n\nInstead we take the vertex type to be the subtype of Finset β consisting of\nk-element subsets, i.e. {s : Finset β // s.card = k}. This means we no longer\nneed to assume Fintype β but we lose direct access to the Finset.powersetCard\nAPI (even when β is finite). However we can solve this issue with a simple\ntranslation lemma saying that, for any predicate p, counting the k-element\nsubsets satisfying p as terms of the subtype {s : Finset β // s.card = k} gives\nthe same answer as counting them as members of the family\n(Finset.univ : Finset β).powersetCard k. (See\nIndependence Number of the Kneser Graph for details.)\n\nThe k-th Kneser graph on β: vertices are k-element subsets of β, and\nedges join disjoint, distinct subsets./-- The k-th Kneser graph on β: vertices are k-element subsets of β, and\nedges join disjoint, distinct subsets. -/\n@[simps]\ndef kneserGraph (β : Type*) (k : ℕ) :\n    SimpleGraph {s : Finset β // s.card = k} where\n  Adj u v := Disjoint u.val v.val ∧ u ≠ v\n  symm := ⟨fun _ _ h => ⟨h.1.symm, h.2.symm⟩⟩\n\n\n#check kneserGraph_adj -- (kneserGraph β k).Adj u v = (Disjoint ↑u ↑v ∧ u ≠ v)\n\n\ninstance (β : Type*) (k : ℕ) [DecidableEq β] :\n    DecidableRel (kneserGraph β k).Adj := fun u v => by\n  unfold kneserGraph; infer_instance\n\n\nWe introduce the notation Kn[n, k] for the k-th Kneser graph on Fin n.\n\nKn[n, k] is the Kneser graph kneserGraph (Fin n) k: its vertices are the\nk-element subsets of Fin n, and edges join disjoint, distinct subsets./-- Kn[n, k] is the Kneser graph kneserGraph (Fin n) k: its vertices are the\nk-element subsets of Fin n, and edges join disjoint, distinct subsets. -/\nscoped notation \"Kn[\" n \", \" k \"]\" => kneserGraph (Fin n) k\n\n\nA special case of the Kneser graph is the Petersen graph J. Petersen, Sur le théorème de Tait [On Tait's theorem], L'Intermédiaire des Mathématiciens 5 (1898), 225–227.,\nwhich is Kn[5, 2].\n\nThe Petersen graph which is the Kneser graph Kn[5, 2]./-- The *Petersen* graph which is the Kneser graph Kn[5, 2]. -/\nabbrev petersenGraph : SimpleGraph {s : Finset (Fin 5) // s.card = 2} := Kn[5, 2]\n\n\nThe Petersen graph as the Kneser graph Kn(5,2)\n\nWe will look at the independence number of Kn[n, k] in Cliques\nand colourings of Kn[n, k] in Exercises Colorings. Computing its\nindependence number is really just a case of translating the Erdős–Ko–Rado theorem\n(which is in Mathlib) into the language of SimpleGraph.\n\nend SimpleGraph\n\n\nnamespace SimpleGraph\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Constructions","header":"3.7. Kneser Graphs*","id":"/Constructions/#section-kneser-graphs"}});